control 'CKV_AWS_382' do
  title 'Ensure no security groups allow egress from 0.0.0.0:0 to port -1'

  desc <<~DESC
    Checkov asserts this against Terraform. This profile asserts it against
    the aws_security_group resources that actually exist, enumerated through
    the declarative API spec.

    Checkov only sees the egress block if the HCL wrote one. A security
    group whose Terraform declares no egress at all is silently given the
    open default by EC2, and reading the deployed permissions is the only
    way that appears.
  DESC

  desc 'rationale', <<~RATIONALE
    Unrestricted egress is what turns a foothold into an exfiltration
    channel and a command-and-control channel; the boundary that matters
    after a compromise is the one on the way out. Rated medium because it is
    the AWS default rather than an act, so the finding is nearly universal
    in an account that has not done egress design, and a universal finding
    is a backlog item rather than an incident.
  RATIONALE

  desc 'check', <<~CHECK
    Checkov looks for: aws_security_group: an EGRESS permission with
    IpProtocol "-1" whose IpRanges contain 0.0.0.0/0 or whose Ipv6Ranges
    contain ::/0.
  CHECK

  desc 'fix', <<~'FIX'
    Terraform — aws_security_group:

      resource "aws_security_group" "app" {
        name   = "app"
        vpc_id = aws_vpc.example.id

        # An egress block MUST be present. A security group whose Terraform
        # declares none is given the open "-1 to 0.0.0.0/0" default by EC2, and
        # the HCL looks clean while the deployed group is wide open.
        egress {
          description     = "to the database"
          from_port       = 5432
          to_port         = 5432
          protocol        = "tcp"
          security_groups = [aws_security_group.db.id]
        }

        egress {
          description     = "to AWS APIs via the VPC endpoints"
          from_port       = 443
          to_port         = 443
          protocol        = "tcp"
          prefix_list_ids = [aws_vpc_endpoint.s3.prefix_list_id]
        }
      }

    Out of band — aws_security_group:

      aws ec2 authorize-security-group-egress --group-id <sg-id> --ip-permissions IpProtocol=tcp,FromPort=443,ToPort=443,PrefixListIds=[{PrefixListId=<pl-id>}]
      aws ec2 revoke-security-group-egress --group-id <sg-id> --ip-permissions IpProtocol=-1,IpRanges=[{CidrIp=0.0.0.0/0}]

    Note (aws_security_group): Order matters here in a way it does not for
    ingress. Authorize the replacement egress FIRST and revoke the open default
    second — revoking first severs every outbound connection the workload has,
    including the ones to AWS APIs it needs to keep running. Egress narrowing is
    the change most likely to break a working system, so establish what the
    workload actually talks to from VPC flow logs before doing it, and expect to
    need endpoints or prefix lists for AWS services.
  FIX

